 ## Background
-Hash-grid encoding was introduced in Instant-NGP to accelerate NeRF training and rendering by replacing positional encoding with multi-resolution hash tables [13315-instant-ngp]. The concept of using hash tables for neural radiance fields was further explored in ZipNeRF, which utilized large hash tables to avoid collisions and support unbounded scenes [S1]. However, ZipNeRF was observed to incorrectly place multiple copies of objects close to the camera in sparse settings [S1]. Simple-RF addressed this by introducing an augmented model with reduced hash table capacity and domain constraints to regularize the main model [S1]. Anti-aliasing techniques for hash grids were later developed to suppress high-frequency artifacts, with methods like Nexels proposing down-weighting factors derived from pixel footprint statistics [S3].
+Hash-grid encoding was introduced in Instant-NGP to accelerate NeRF training and rendering by replacing positional encoding with multi-resolution hash tables [G]. The concept of using hash tables for neural radiance fields was further explored in ZipNeRF, which can utilize large hash tables to avoid collisions, but its augmented variant reduces table size to enforce feature sharing [S1]. Simple-RF addressed sparse-view floaters by introducing an auxiliary model with reduced capacity and domain constraints [S1]. Anti-aliasing techniques for hash grids were later developed to suppress high-frequency artifacts, with methods like Nexels proposing down-weighting factors derived from pixel footprint statistics [S3].
 
